WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.000
 The most powerful
writing often

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.550
comes from confronting taboos.

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:08.020
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:11.490
All those subjects relate to
many people who have no outlet.

00:00:15.258 --> 00:00:19.190
As a writer, if one can face
the darkest elements in oneself

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:20.950
and the things that
are secret, you

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.680
have such a feeling of power.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.060
In this class,
you're going to be

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.090
exploring your own
imagination deep within you.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:30.190
And we're going to begin
with shorter forms,

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:33.400
because short stories, short
monologues, poetry, things

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.430
that you can finish
and show other people--

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:39.970
that's very satisfying and
necessary for a writer.

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:42.400
What we all need
is the satisfaction

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.640
of this little uplift that
we get psychologically

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:48.360
from finishing something.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.250
There are two ways of
looking at writing.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.610
One way is that you're telling
a story very transparently.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:55.980
The other is that you're
telling a story with language,

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:57.750
and-- and language
is the point.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.330
How fast do you want
your story to read?

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:03.420
If it reads too fast, then
it might be superficial.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.730
A person reads it in
five minutes, it's all over.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:13.050
So we're also going to be
exploring a writing workshop.

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:14.850
Writers are like cooks that--

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.530
they keep everything
in the refrigerator

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:17.770
and put it all
in the casserole.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:19.860
Like, what doesn't go
in for dinner tonight,

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.090
well, it's going to
show up next Sunday.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:25.440
So your story has a few
too many bits in it.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.420
I've always felt that
art is the highest

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.910
expression of the human spirit.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.740
Art is the way that we
communicate with one another.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:35.040
So if anyone who's
listening to me

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.970
feels that he or she is a
writer, you probably are.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:43.120
Everybody has at least
one story to tell.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:44.440
Writing should be pleasurable.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:45.440
It should be fun.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:46.750
It should be exploratory.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.780
You should be writing about
things that surprise you.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:53.440
I think that is the instinct
for all kinds of art.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:57.100
All you need to do, really,
is to take that instinct

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.220
and transform it into a craft.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:05.320
I'm Joyce Carol Oates, and
this is my MasterClass.

